// A host that names no address — a name the resolver refuses, and the empty
// name — raises a catchable NetworkError from listen, connect and
// connect_within (spec/05-stdlib.md, connect, connect_within, listen).
//
// The listener makes the empty-name cases discriminating. An empty name
// resolves to the loopback address on a JVM, so a backend that binds or
// connects it succeeds against this listener and prints "no error", while
// the C library's getaddrinfo resolves no address for it. The name "a b"
// holds a space, so no resolver answers it.
//
// The second part names each spelling that two resolvers answer differently.
// Which spellings resolve, and which address each names, is
// implementation-defined (spec/README.md, Conformance, Host-name resolution),
// so that part binds no answer of the resolver: the call either succeeds or
// raises a catchable NetworkError, and it prints a line only when a backend
// fails another way — an uncaught fault fails the probe, and another kind
// prints its name. Both directions of the difference are covered, measured
// with listen, connect and connect_within on glibc 2.39 and on a JVM:
//
//   host                   native, QBE, bootstrap   JVM
//   "*"                    wildcard bind, and       NetworkError
//                          connect to the loopback
//                          addresses
//   "0x7f000001"           127.0.0.1                NetworkError
//   "0177.0.0.1"           127.0.0.1 (octal)        177.0.0.1 (decimal)
//   "[::1]"                NetworkError             ::1
//   "[::ffff:127.0.0.1]"   NetworkError             ::ffff:127.0.0.1
//   "[::]"                 NetworkError             the wildcard address
//   "::1%lo"               NetworkError             ::1 on lo
//
// The octal spelling is the one case whose loop below names two calls and not
// three. Each resolver reads the same digits its own way, and both calls end
// quickly: the C library binds 127.0.0.1, and a JVM fails to bind 177.0.0.1 at
// once because no local address names it, and ends connect_within at its own
// limit (1 s) because 177.0.0.1 does not answer. A plain connect has no limit, so on
// the JVM it waits for the operating system's limit on a connection attempt
// (136 s measured). 05-stdlib does not bind that limit and item 6, Speed,
// covers it, so a case here would fail the differential gate's own clock and
// never an answer of the language.
//
// "localhost" differs the same way in the order of its addresses, which the
// same rule covers (docs/decisions/blocking-call-suspension.md, "connect and
// name resolution"; issue #1095).
// STATUS: ok
// CHECK: listen with an unresolved name: NetworkError
// CHECK: listen with an empty name: NetworkError
// CHECK: connect with an unresolved name: NetworkError
// CHECK: connect with an empty name: NetworkError
// CHECK: connect_within with an unresolved name: NetworkError
// CHECK: connect_within with an empty name: NetworkError
// CHECK: spellings checked: 6
// CHECK: octal spelling checked: 2
// CHECK: after
var srv = listen("127.0.0.1", 0);
var port = srv.port();
var bad = "a b";

try {
    var s = listen(bad, 0);
    print "listen with an unresolved name: no error";
    s.close();
} catch (e) {
    print "listen with an unresolved name: " + e.kind;
}

try {
    var s = listen("", 0);
    print "listen with an empty name: no error";
    s.close();
} catch (e) {
    print "listen with an empty name: " + e.kind;
}

try {
    var c = connect(bad, port);
    print "connect with an unresolved name: no error";
    c.close();
} catch (e) {
    print "connect with an unresolved name: " + e.kind;
}

try {
    var c = connect("", port);
    print "connect with an empty name: no error";
    c.close();
} catch (e) {
    print "connect with an empty name: " + e.kind;
}

try {
    var c = connect_within(bad, port, 1);
    print "connect_within with an unresolved name: no error";
    c.close();
} catch (e) {
    print "connect_within with an unresolved name: " + e.kind;
}

try {
    var c = connect_within("", port, 1);
    print "connect_within with an empty name: no error";
    c.close();
} catch (e) {
    print "connect_within with an empty name: " + e.kind;
}

var spellings = ["*", "0x7f000001", "[::1]", "[::ffff:127.0.0.1]", "[::]",
                 "::1%lo"];
var checked = 0;

for (var host in spellings) {
    checked = checked + 1;

    try {
        var s = listen(host, 0);
        s.close();
    } catch (e) {
        if (e.kind != "NetworkError") print "listen " + host + ": " + e.kind;
    }

    try {
        var c = connect(host, port);
        c.close();
    } catch (e) {
        if (e.kind != "NetworkError") print "connect " + host + ": " + e.kind;
    }

    try {
        var c = connect_within(host, port, 1);
        c.close();
    } catch (e) {
        if (e.kind != "NetworkError") {
            print "connect_within " + host + ": " + e.kind;
        }
    }
}

print "spellings checked: " + str(checked);

try {
    var s = listen("0177.0.0.1", 0);
    s.close();
} catch (e) {
    if (e.kind != "NetworkError") print "listen 0177.0.0.1: " + e.kind;
}

try {
    var c = connect_within("0177.0.0.1", port, 1);
    c.close();
} catch (e) {
    if (e.kind != "NetworkError") {
        print "connect_within 0177.0.0.1: " + e.kind;
    }
}

print "octal spelling checked: 2";

srv.close();
print "after";
